<Project>

  <!--
    Central Package Management for the .NET port.

    Every NuGet version used by QuickStat.Core / QuickStat.App / QuickStat.Tests is pinned here and
    ONLY here.  A <PackageReference> in a .csproj must never carry a Version attribute.

    The dependency list is deliberately curated (PORT-PLAN.md §1.1): CommunityToolkit.Mvvm,
    Microsoft.Data.SqlClient, Microsoft.Extensions.*, ClosedXML, Serilog, xUnit.  No commercial
    control suites, no additional libraries without a plan change.

    SERILOG WAS SUCH A PLAN CHANGE, made deliberately on 2026-08-27 and recorded in PORT-PLAN.md
    §1.1.  Docs/Port/01-data-access.md §7.5 offered the choice - "a small custom ILoggerProvider (or
    Serilog, if the team prefers a dependency)" - and the port took the custom route, largely to keep
    the log file byte-compatible with the Delphi's plaintext format.  The product owner withdrew that
    requirement: the format is not worth preserving, only what is logged, when and how.  That removed
    the only argument for hand-rolling daily rolling, retention, shared-file access and encoding, all
    of which Serilog.Sinks.File already does, and the three hand-rolled files are deleted.

    IT SITS BEHIND Microsoft.Extensions.Logging, NOT IN FRONT OF IT.  QuickStat.Core takes
    Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions and nothing else; every call site logs through
    ILogger<T>.  Serilog appears only under QuickStat.App/Logging/ - not even App.xaml.cs names a
    Serilog type - so replacing it again is a change to one directory.  (FastTrak.PersonInfoSync
    takes the other route, a global using Serilog in every project; that is the one thing
    deliberately not copied from it, because it would put a third-party logging type in
    QuickStat.Core's public surface.)
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <!--
      Transitive pinning off: it would promote every transitive dependency of Microsoft.Data.SqlClient
      and ClosedXML into a direct, version-locked reference and make routine upgrades painful.
    -->
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>false</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Application / library dependencies -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="ClosedXML" Version="0.105.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="CommunityToolkit.Mvvm" Version="8.4.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" Version="7.0.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.11" />

    <!--
      Logging.  Versions match FastTrak.PersonInfoSync (Serilog 4.3.1, Sinks.File 7.0.0) so the two
      DIPS C# applications do not drift apart on the same dependency.  Serilog.Sinks.Console is
      deliberately NOT taken: a WinExe has no console, and debugger output already comes from
      Microsoft.Extensions.Logging's own AddDebug provider.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog" Version="4.3.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.Extensions.Logging" Version="9.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.Sinks.File" Version="7.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Test stack: xunit v2 on VSTest.  READ BEFORE "UPGRADING" TO xunit.v3.

    xunit.v3 was tried first and rejected.  It is a Microsoft.Testing.Platform (MTP) framework, and
    MTP orchestration under "dotnet test" is broken on this SDK (10.0.400): the suite reports
    "Zero tests ran" / exit code 5 even though the test executable itself discovers and passes every
    test when run directly.  Established by experiment, not by guesswork:

      * a bare, minimal xunit.v3 project outside this repo fails identically, so it is not our
        configuration;
      * xunit.v3 3.1.0 fails the same way as 4.0.0, so it is not a version regression;
      * removing UseWPF / net10.0-windows / IsTestProject changes nothing;
      * the other MTP entry point, <TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport>, is dead on .NET 10 - it
        errors with "Testing with VSTest target is no longer supported ...";
      * the same minimal project on xunit v2 + VSTest passes.

    "dotnet build && dotnet test" passing is an acceptance criterion (PORT-PLAN.md §10.1) and is
    what every implementation agent and any future CI will run, so the mature stack wins.  Nothing
    this port needs - facts, theories, golden-file and byte-comparison assertions - requires v3.

    Revisit when MTP's "dotnet test" support stabilises; it is a package swap, not a rewrite.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
